// Regression test for issue #242: a syntax error TWO import-levels deep
// (this file imports nested_broken_import_level_b_module.tr, which itself
// imports the already-broken broken_extern_param_module.tr -- see
// fail_import_syntax_error.tr for the single-level version of this repro)
// must produce a clean compile error, not crash the compiler.
//
// Before the fix, ParseFile() forced g_recoverActive false for its entire
// duration specifically to avoid a DIFFERENT crash: any TurfError raised
// two or more import-levels deep would longjmp() straight past this
// function's own LexerStateGuard/ImportStackGuard (longjmp never runs C++
// destructors for stack objects between the jump and its setjmp() point),
// permanently leaving the global SourceFile pointer aimed at an already-
// destroyed local std::ifstream -- confirmed at the time as a heap-
// corruption crash (`munmap_chunk(): invalid pointer`). The old fix traded
// that crash for calling std::exit() on ANY error inside an import, at ANY
// depth -- safe, but meant a broken import always killed the whole
// process, fatal for a tool embedding this compiler in-process (an LSP)
// rather than shelling out per compile.
//
// Fixed by giving ParseFile() its own LOCAL recovery boundary (its own
// setjmp(), armed in the same function scope as its guards) instead of
// relying on a distant caller's -- verified live under AddressSanitizer
// (zero corruption) with this exact 2-level-deep repro, plus confirming
// the process survives and can still successfully compile something else
// afterward. This file is the compile-time regression coverage for that
// fix; the crash class itself needs sanitizer instrumentation to prove
// absence of corruption (not something test_runner.sh's pass/fail check
// alone can assert), so it was verified manually, not here.
import nested_broken_import_level_b_module;

fn main(): int {
    return 0;
}
